# 08 - the tun document names no outbound interface; the tunnel excludes this app's uid
#
# The device refused every start with `app/proxyman/inbound: failed to start proxy > route
# ip+net: netlinkrib: permission denied`. That line comes from proxy/tun/handler.go: with
# `autoOutboundsInterface` set, Handler.Start resolves the tun's index and then the interface
# by name, and `net.InterfaceByName` reads the interface table over rtnetlink, which Android
# denies to an app uid. There is no name to give the core that avoids that read, and the
# setting is not what keeps the core's own transport out of its tunnel anyway: on Android the
# VpnService states that per uid. So the key is gone from the document, and the service adds
# its own package to `addDisallowedApplication` in the Builder that establishes the tunnel:
# the system then installs no route into the tun for this uid, which is what
# `VpnService.protect` does for one socket and needs no interface name at all. The plumbing
# that only existed to feed the key (the `XrayTun.interfaceName` field, `outboundInterface()`
# and the "no interface, no start" veto) goes with it, and access control no longer puts this
# app on an allow list: a uid cannot be on both, and this one has to stay outside.

